WEBVTT

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.290
Yeah, he's dead. Have a look.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.500
It's clearly a murder.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.080
Murder?!

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:41.370
So, Liam, you have nothing to do
before we reach London, do you?

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.540
Shall we have a contest to see
who can find the culprit first?

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.410
Wh-What are you talking about?!
This is a murder case, not a game!

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.410
Very well. That might be interesting.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.330
However, the case appears to be
a bit more complicated.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:57.450
Huh?

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.660
John...

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.500
Right, what's all the fuss?!

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.330
The transport police?

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.950
<i>The mist is hiding the sin</i>

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.700
<i>I hold in my hand the LIST of judgment</i>

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.330
<i>I'm gonna do the perfect crime</i>
<i>and bury them in a cist</i>

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:39.580
<i>That's my DYING WISH for you</i>
<i>(Set a thief to catch another thief)</i>

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:45.000
<i>Guys of the privileged class torment the weak</i>
<i>(Set a thief to catch another thief)</i>

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.080
<i>And they enjoy it</i>

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.620
<i>That's a bloody world, an endlessly</i>
<i>discriminatory society (dystopia)</i>

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.660
<i>Happiness does not bloom here</i>
<i>(Set a thief to catch another thief)</i>

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.790
<i>We are oppressed</i>

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:07.370
<i>We must do something. Come on</i>

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:13.830
<i>Let the end begin</i>

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:19.660
<i>Break it to relieve your soul</i>

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:25.580
<i>Open your eyes. You'll be reborn</i>
<i>when you lose your life</i>

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:30.660
<i>We're lords of crime. We're sacrifice for ideals</i>

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:42.950
<i>Freedom lives forever, even if we die</i>

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:50.790
Wha--?!

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.040
"The Two Detectives Act 2"

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.040
What is the meaning of this?!

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.410
"The Two Detectives Act 2"

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.410
The Great Northern Railway
Transport Police are in charge of this!

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.750
Take this man into custody!

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.790
Aye!

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.620
Just a moment, please! This blood isn't--

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.830
Someone ran into me a moment ago
as I was leaving the loo!

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.120
I went back to my compartment,
and after a short while, I noticed the blood.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.500
Please, you must believe me!

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.540
We'll listen to what you have to say
for yourself later. Now, come along!

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.750
Just a moment, please.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.540
I am Lestrade of Scotland Yard.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.910
Eh? You're the one with the
reputation for competence!

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:26.750
Yes, well...

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.950
This is consulting detective Sherlock Holmes.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.660
The famous detective?

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:37.410
And the man whom you've cast your suspicion
of murder upon is his friend, Dr John H. Watson.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.500
H-He is?!

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.080
Dr Watson is not the sort of man
who would commit murder.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.450
Please, exercise wise judgement here.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.660
Still, under the circumstances...

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.870
Aah! This is a waste of time!

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:56.410
<i>You're </i>the ones who don't understand
the circumstances, you bollock-brains!

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:58.830
Wh-What do you mean by that?!

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.790
See here! The murderer
is still aboard this train!

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:08.250
Unless he's leapt from a train travelling
to London at 100 kph, that is!

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:12.330
The next station this train will stop at
after leaving Doncaster will be at Grantham.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.790
That's about 80 kilometres away.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:15.500
In other words--

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.040
That's roughly 48 minutes.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.750
Exactly! We have to find the killer
in 48 minutes, or he'll get away!

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.080
Can you people do that?! Well?!

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.290
Er... Well, that is...

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.040
But it <i>is </i>something that we can do.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.790
Holmes has actually solved a good many cases.
Why don't we leave this up to him?

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.660
I don't care how famous a detective he is,
turning this over to a civilian is...

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.200
Brother, we have nothing at all
to do with this incident.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.290
Best that we not become involved with Holmes.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.290
You're right. But even so...

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:57.660
...an innocent man has fallen
under the suspicion of murder...

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.410
...and it will be quite impossible
for the transport police

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.330
to catch the murderer and prove
their crime in the time remaining.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:09.950
Why not fulfil our noblesse oblige
in this matter, Louis?

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.080
Very well, Brother.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:19.580
I'm telling you, just give us 48 minutes
and we'll solve this whole thing!

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.330
Right, Liam?

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.040
"Liam"? How familiar.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.450
Why, there may be a way to solve this
without even taking that much time.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.330
Ho? Listen to you.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.160
Chance may have brought us together here,

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.000
but I will give my all to ensure that
the Moriarty name is not put to shame.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.200
Sounds good. Then it's a contest to see
who can deliver the culprit first.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.660
This isn't a game.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:50.660
Very well. On the good word
of Inspector Lestrade,

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.040
I will leave it to you until
we reach the next station.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.540
Much obliged.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.620
However, we will still place
Mr Watson in our custody.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.080
Sherlo--

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.040
Time is short. Let's do this quickly, Liam.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.250
I was hoping you would say that.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:23.000
H-Hey, you don't have a key!
How did you open that?

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.160
You're a policeman, aren't you?
Can't you even pick a lock?

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:48.410
So that's it.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:49.950
I just about have it.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.750
Did you find something that
will lead us to the killer?

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.870
Yeah. What we know at this point...

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:01.160
...is that the victim is a jeweller
from London, Trevor Redwood.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:07.410
The killer is a male... who boarded
the train at the terminus in Durham...

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.790
...and when his drugging-robbery went awry,
he knifed this man to death.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:20.450
A drugging-robbery?
Then this is not just an ordinary murder?

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.080
Unmistakably.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:24.910
Isn't that obvious?

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.540
It's almost as if there are <i>two </i>Holmeses!

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:33.160
My word, this man Moriarty, as well...

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.450
Please, would you explain it to me?

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.540
What am I going to do with you?

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.500
Time is short, so I'm only
going to say this once.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:48.040
Look here. First, this compartment
has two sets of men's footprints.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.870
One set belongs to the victim;

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:53.750
the other from the Durham man,
measuring 10 inches.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.830
How do you know he was from Durham?

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.950
Look at the shoes of the maths professor
from Durham University.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:02.080
Hmm? The mud?

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.000
Yes. It was raining in Durham.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.500
But the weather has been clear
since our first stop, in York.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:14.080
Indeed. That does narrow down the sex
and the station where the killer boarded.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.870
Additionally, this is what
Mr Holmes was talking about.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:25.450
Whisky. The bottle is here, as well.

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:28.660
There is still a faint,
lingering aroma of chloral.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:34.750
When mixed with alcohol, it becomes a dangerous
drug which exhibits powerful soporific effects.

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:39.620
Whatever means were used,
someone must have laced his drink with it.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:43.040
Then why did the murderer kill the victim?

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:46.910
Huh? Because something happened
which the killer wasn't expecting.

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:49.080
Nothing more than that.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.830
This was emphatically a sudden,
unforeseen crime.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.500
If the objective were to kill this man,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.910
there is no reason to do so on a train,
where a quick escape is impossible.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.290
It is more likely that the culprit
drugged the victim,

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:06.950
and despite his success in
momentarily incapacitating him...

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.910
...the drug may not have been strong enough,
or his tolerance to it too high,

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:16.700
resulting in his awakening during the theft.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:20.660
That must have been unexpected.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.120
Judging by the shape of his wound,

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.540
the weapon must have been
a keen blade, such as a knife.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:32.040
The marks left on the case indicate that he
tried to pry it open with just such an item.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:36.790
A knife, eh? I don't see one here.
The killer must have left with it.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:39.120
The chances are quite high.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:44.790
When the victim awakened, there was
a scuffle, during which he stabbed him.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:47.040
Perhaps he was surprised by the amount of blood,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:51.750
but there are indications that the killer held
his hand over the wound to stop the bleeding.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:57.200
Quite so. There is no blood on the victim's
hands, but there is a bloody handprint there.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:00.790
He must have panicked and
forgotten his primary objective.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:03.200
He ended up leaving the body here,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.660
and even the whisky, and the
briefcase he was after still remain.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:10.620
"Take the weapon and the room key,
and lock the door"--

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:14.250
It looks as though no other
thought entered his mind.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:18.290
The crime scene vividly demonstrates
that the culprit was flustered.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.620
Agreed, Liam.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.580
You're done with the crime scene?

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:32.330
Yeah. There's nothing left to see.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:33.830
Let's go, John--

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.450
Thirty-nine minutes remain.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.870
Lord Moriarty!

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:48.830
I brought the passenger list
and compartment layouts!

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:53.450
I will take a look at them.
But first, I have a few questions for you.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:11:02.120
Holmes... the one man in the world to
whom Brother William pays due respect.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:06.660
However, from Brother's standpoint,
he is no more than a pawn to be used.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:13.250
And yet he addresses him so casually,
not to mention challenges him to a contest.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.870
Could he be any cheekier?

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:19.040
If he ever stops being of use to us, I'll...

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.620
I see...

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:24.250
Very well, then. Thank you.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:27.580
No, not at all. Assisting a nobleman...

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:31.160
...is an honour.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.410
Have you learnt something?

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:36.660
Yes.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:40.700
Holmes will use the particular
circumstantial evidence,

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:43.910
such as who could have gotten him to drink,
and the footprints left behind,

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:47.200
to narrow down who the culprit might be.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.450
And since I can, I think I will
go about it another way--

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:55.580
by putting together a psychological
profile of the killer's mind.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:11.950
The culprit was greatly upset
by the unforeseen killing.

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:18.370
Even so, the reason why he
locked the door as he left

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:21.870
was because he had at least some fear
of his crime being revealed.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:26.080
Then the culprit bumped into
Mr Watson at the washroom.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:30.410
Louis, do you know why the killer
went to the washroom?

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:36.830
The killer had to have been carrying
the weapon and the room key.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:41.450
Having them on him would be like
declaring that he was the murderer.

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:44.500
So he went there to dispose of them, didn't he?

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.660
Quite right. That is one reason.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:50.410
The windows in the passenger
compartments do not open,

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:53.250
so he probably threw them
out the washroom window.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:56.700
Once the tracks are searched, they will
likely find the key and the weapon.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.330
Then, what is the other reason?

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.330
Simple, Louis.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:05.870
Because he wanted to wash the blood off.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.160
The blood?

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:08.870
The blood from his victim?

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:13.910
If he tossed out his clothing
along with the weapon and key...

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:18.700
Yes. He should have tossed them, but he did not.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:21.660
The killer is someone who could
not dispose of his clothes.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:25.160
Which is why he had no choice but to wash them.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.410
Holmes determined that the killer
was male based on his footprints,

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:31.830
but he did not specify who
it was any further than that.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:37.660
However, he will soon realise that those
footprints will be found everywhere.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:42.080
Whilst the sizes may differ, there are
countless identical footprints about.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.500
And they have but one source.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:50.540
The shoes that are issued to the crewmen.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.160
A crewman?

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:59.250
Then it's only natural that he would offer
a drink to a first-class passenger.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:01.700
Nor could he get rid of his jacket.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.410
Let's not forget his gloves, either.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:04.700
Huh?

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.580
Their white gloves are issued to them,
and are individually monogrammed.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.790
Meaning that if he rid himself
of his jacket and gloves,

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:15.250
he would as much as be confessing to the murder.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:19.750
The killer went to the washroom to wash off
the blood from his gloves and jacket

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:23.080
and ended up bumping into Mr Watson.

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:25.910
Should we look for this crewman right now?

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.120
There are six crewmen aboard this train.

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:35.450
Once Holmes reaches this conclusion,
he will gather them up for us.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:37.580
Before he does, though...

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:41.790
...I need to meet with each one,
as there's something I must first do.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.200
Just to be sure.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:03.870
...and that is the quick outline
of the crime, as Liam and I see it.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.290
Indeed.

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:07.660
So the man who could not throw out
his blood-stained clothes,

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:12.290
and had no choice but to wash them out
in the loo, will be our killer, then?

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:15.450
Yeah. Which means...

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.830
...the killer is one of the crewmen.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:19.870
Our investigation has reached its conclusion.

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:24.410
The two of you who have a shoe size
of 10 inches, take one step forward.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:30.120
Now, let's examine the gloves on each of you.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.120
O-Our gloves, sir?

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:33.660
Let me see.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:40.290
Very good. Next.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:45.040
Hmm? Your sleeves are wet.
Would you open your hands for me?

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.080
Open your hands!

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.250
Bingo.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:07.080
Passenger Car Attendant Eddie Hawthorne,
you are the killer.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.160
Eddie, how could you?!

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:11.160
--Impossible!
--You're so diligent, though!

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:12.370
It couldn't be...

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:17.040
Mr Watson, it appears there's been a
misunderstanding. Please excuse us--

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:20.580
You're wrong! I did not kill anyone!

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.500
This blood is... it's... from when...
from when<i> I </i>got hurt!

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:28.120
When <i>you </i>got hurt? Then take
your gloves off and show me!

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:29.500
Sure.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:36.910
What the...?

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:41.330
Earlier, whilst I was working,
I cut myself through my gloves,

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:44.330
and I haven't sought attention yet.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:47.160
Please, you must believe me! It's the truth!

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:53.120
Unless, you have any other proof?

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.160
He's clearly prevaricating, Lestrade.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.040
I know. But there's nothing else
that implicates him.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:03.660
And even after we recover the weapon
from the side of the tracks,

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.950
if he claims it doesn't belong to him...

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:09.080
This certainly is odd...

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:15.200
Liam...?

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:18.620
You say that you hurt yourself
<i>through </i>your gloves, yes?

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:19.830
Y-Yes.

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:24.500
Then tell me, how did you have blood
fly up onto your eyeglasses?

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:25.700
Eh?!

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:29.330
Ah, well...

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:32.660
Injuring your palms,
whilst wearing gloves, no less,

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:35.450
should leave no blood stains up there.

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:39.700
That ended up there when you killed
the man in that compartment.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.580
No, I...

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:45.160
And that one is not all.
There are more, as well.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:49.330
Would you like me to point out each one?

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:55.910
It said on the passenger manifest
that he was a jeweller...

00:17:55.910 --> 00:18:00.370
I never meant to kill him... I never meant to...

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:09.290
Eddie Hawthorne, you are under
arrest on suspicion of murder!

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:16.870
Good. We arrive at Grantham in three minutes.
We made it in time, Mr Holmes.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:21.870
Liam, you rascal... You're even better
at bluffing than I am, aren't you?

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.200
Why, whatever do you mean?

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:28.120
Well, we'll excuse ourselves now.

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:31.040
Yeah, let's grab something to eat sometime.

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:34.750
Is this destiny?

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:40.950
Yet another man, with a brain to rival that
of Holmes's, here as his contemporary...

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.000
Moriarty...

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:52.330
I'm impressed that you spotted
a blood stain that small, Brother.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:55.040
Yes, well, it was <i>my </i>blood.

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:55.910
Huh?

00:18:55.910 --> 00:19:00.370
I anticipated that the killer
would try to be evasive.

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:04.910
Which is why, before Holmes called them
together, I contacted each of them,

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:09.370
and came up with pretences to plant
my blood on them in various places.

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:15.040
Which means that if you hadn't been there,

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:17.660
the resolution to the case
would have come much later.

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:21.370
Then Holmes's endgame really is weak, isn't it?

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:26.250
Holmes's method would have been
plenty to prove his guilt at trial.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:30.290
On the other hand, the method
I employed is outside the law.

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:35.660
He is too trusting. But that is
not necessarily a bad thing.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:39.580
That is one thing I like about him.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:43.870
Moreover, I think those two
make a very good pair.

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:56.040
Ironic, isn't it?

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:59.540
I told you to go ahead and become a murderer,

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:05.950
but if it weren't for you, right now,
<i>I </i>would be the one branded a killer.

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:10.370
Uh, John... I, uh...

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:12.620
How do I say this?

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:16.790
Lately, things haven't been going
my way, you know? And, um...

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:21.370
...a-as long as you're okay with it,
I wouldn't mind us teaming up again.

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:26.830
Is that your idea of an apology?

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:32.370
So the great detective is still a mere mortal.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:37.290
Oi, John, what does that mean?
Are we a team again, or aren't we?

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.370
Give me one of your cigarettes, Sherlock.

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:44.080
And what do you know?
You don't have any matches, do you?

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:48.700
Without me around, how would
you ever light your cigarettes?

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:54.040
You've gotten so you can make jokes, John!

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:57.910
That's the way my partner ought to be!

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.660
Begging your pardon...

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:38.200
...but I am here to make my report...

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:40.290
...Your Majesty.

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:45.450
Thank you for coming.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:49.120
Your Majesty, the situation is dire.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:53.450
The whereabouts of the stolen documents
are, unfortunately, unknown.

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:58.750
There have yet to be any demands
or contact from whomever has them.

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:05.620
Those documents detail one of
Great Britain's greatest secrets.

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:10.040
You are free to use whatever means
you must, no matter how ruthless.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.080
Get those documents back...

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:15.080
...Sir Mycroft Holmes.

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:21.620
Yes, Your Majesty.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:26.200
<i>An oath committed by souls assumed,</i>

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:30.410
<i>with ideals that wallow in genetic karma</i>

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:49.540
<i>Lie! Be resolute if you're going</i>
<i>to share your pain</i>

00:22:49.540 --> 00:22:51.790
<i>(Demonstrate your resolve)</i>

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:55.290
<i>Like, dislike, you can't afford</i>
<i>to care either way</i>

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:58.500
<i>It depends on the objective</i>
<i>(Making it happen is what's most important)</i>

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:02.000
<i>The necessary evil of self-sacrifice</i>

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:05.620
<i>is a righteous cause that no one can comprehend</i>

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:09.370
<i>Take pride in the inviolable bonds shared</i>

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:14.200
<i>among brothers who bear the same crime</i>

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:17.870
<i>Make a chance trigger your inevitable stance</i>

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:19.540
<i>and get drunk on your accomplishments</i>

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:21.330
<i>at the end</i>

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.500
<i>Don't believe in what you can see,</i>

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:28.750
<i>in truths there's no point in knowing</i>
<i>(Whatever it takes)</i>

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:35.370
<i>Defer to its own validity,</i>
<i>you only have one goal</i>

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:36.870
<i>(Let me hear you say)</i>

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:43.700
<i>You can't lie to yourself, come along with me</i>

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:49.330
<i>It's all because there is no answer</i>

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:51.700
<i>There is no turning back</i>
